\details{
The learner engine packages are installed with \code{funcml}, so the advertised
registry is intended to be available after a standard installation.

Predictor encoding: most learners receive the one-hot model matrix built
from \code{formula}. Tree learners whose engines split on factors themselves
(\code{ranger}, \code{rpart}, \code{randomForest}, \code{gbm}, \code{C50}, \code{adaboost}, \code{ctree},
\code{cforest}) receive a data frame instead, with factor and character
predictors kept as factors whose levels are fixed from the training data
(logical predictors become 0/1). Prediction applies the same levels, so new
data may lack some levels but may not add unseen ones. These learners fall
back to the model matrix when the formula has interactions or matrix terms
(for example \code{poly()}), or a factor has more levels than the engine accepts
(53 for \code{randomForest}, 1024 for \code{gbm}). The encoding used is stored at
\verb{$encoding} (\code{"native"} or \code{"dummy"}). Inside \code{stacking} and
\code{superlearner}, base learners always receive the model matrix.

The \code{stacking} ensemble takes \code{spec = list(learners, learner_specs, meta_model, folds)}. Its meta-learner is fitted on out-of-fold base-learner
predictions from \code{folds}-fold cross-validation (default 5, drawn from the
current random stream, so \code{seed} makes them reproducible); the base
learners are then refitted on all rows for prediction. The out-of-fold
meta-features are stored at \verb{$state$meta_x}. \code{superlearner} works the same
way with a \code{resampling} object instead of \code{folds}.

\code{meta_model} selects the meta-learner. \code{"convex"} (the \code{superlearner}
default) is the convex super learner of van der Laan, Polley and Hubbard
(2007): one weight per base learner, non-negative and summing to one,
chosen to minimise the out-of-fold loss. For regression it minimises the
squared error of the weighted predictions (as \code{method.NNLS} in the
SuperLearner package, but solved under the sum constraint); for a binary
outcome it minimises the log loss of the weighted logits (as
\code{method.CC_nloglik}); for a multiclass outcome it minimises the log loss of
the weighted class-probability vectors. The weights are stored at
\verb{$state$meta_fit$weights}. \code{"glmnet"} (the \code{stacking} default) is a ridge
regression (logistic or multinomial for classification) with an intercept
and penalty 0.001 on the meta-features; its coefficients are unconstrained
and may be negative. \code{"native"} is the same model without penalty.

\code{kknn} takes \code{spec = list(k, distance, kernel)}: \code{k} is the exact number
of neighbours, \code{distance} the Minkowski power and \code{kernel} any kernel
accepted by \code{\link[kknn:kknn]{kknn::kknn()}} (default \code{"optimal"}; for example
\code{"rectangular"} gives an unweighted k-nearest-neighbour vote or mean).
\code{e1071_svm} takes \code{spec = list(cost, gamma, kernel, epsilon)}; \code{epsilon}
(default 0.1) is the width of the insensitive tube in support vector
regression and is ignored for classification.

\code{nnet} takes \code{spec = list(size, decay, maxit, standardize)}. With
\code{standardize = TRUE} (the default) each predictor column is centred and
scaled by its training mean and standard deviation, stored in the fit and
applied again at prediction; \code{standardize = FALSE} gives the raw inputs.

\code{fda} takes \code{spec$method}, the regression method of \code{\link[mda:fda]{mda::fda()}} as a
function or a name in mda (default \code{"polyreg"}, which gives linear
discriminant analysis; for example \code{"mars"}, \code{"bruto"} or \code{"gen.ridge"});
other \code{spec} entries matching arguments of \code{\link[mda:fda]{mda::fda()}} (\code{dimension},
\code{theta}, ...) or of the method (\code{degree}, \code{nk}, \code{dfmax}, \code{lambda}, ...) are
passed on. \code{type = "prob"} returns the posterior class probabilities.

Engine arguments forwarded from \code{spec} when set: \code{ctree} and \code{cforest} pass
any argument of \code{\link[partykit:ctree_control]{partykit::ctree_control()}} (\code{maxdepth}, \code{minsplit},
\code{minbucket}, \code{mincriterion}, \code{alpha}, ...; \code{mtry} stays the \code{cforest}
argument); \code{lightgbm} passes \code{max_bin}, \code{lambda_l1}, \code{lambda_l2} (or its
alias \code{lambda}), \code{min_data_in_leaf}, \code{min_sum_hessian_in_leaf},
\code{min_gain_to_split}, \code{extra_trees}, \code{max_delta_step} and \code{num_threads};
\code{xgboost} passes \code{lambda}, \code{alpha}, \code{gamma}, \code{min_child_weight},
\code{tree_method}, \code{max_bin}, \code{nthread}, \code{max_delta_step}, \code{max_leaves},
\code{grow_policy}, \code{colsample_bylevel}, \code{colsample_bynode} and
\code{scale_pos_weight}. \code{fastgbm} uses \code{spec$threads} threads; by default the
value of the \code{OMP_NUM_THREADS} environment variable, or 1 when it is unset
(\code{threads = 0} uses all cores).

For regression, \code{xgboost} accepts \code{spec$objective}, any xgboost regression
objective (default \code{"reg:squarederror"}), for example \code{"count:poisson"}
for counts, \code{"reg:gamma"} or \code{"reg:tweedie"} (with
\code{spec$tweedie_variance_power}, default 1.5 in xgboost). Predictions are
on the response (mean) scale. Classification objectives are set
automatically.
}
